# mariadb — real-world examples

## Reset the root password (skip-grant-tables method)

```sh
systemctl stop mariadb
mysqld_safe --skip-grant-tables &
mysql -u root
```

```sql
USE mysql;
UPDATE user SET password=PASSWORD("NewPassword") WHERE User='root';
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;
EXIT;
```

```sh
systemctl restart mariadb
mysql -u root -p
```

## Homelab: install and start on Tumbleweed

```sh
sudo zypper install mariadb mariadb-client
sudo systemctl enable --now mariadb
sudo mysql_secure_installation
```

## Homelab: create a database and app user

```sql
CREATE DATABASE homelab_inventory;
CREATE USER 'david'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY 'ChangeMe!';
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON homelab_inventory.* TO 'david'@'localhost';
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;
```

## Homelab: quick backup and restore

```sh
mysqldump -u root -p --all-databases > /srv/backups/mariadb-$(date +%F).sql
mysql -u root -p homelab_inventory < /srv/backups/homelab_inventory.sql
```

## Homelab: check the server is healthy

```sh
systemctl status mariadb
mysqladmin -u root -p status
```

| Command                   | What it does                    |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| mysql -u root -p          | interactive client              |
| mysqldump                 | logical backup                  |
| mysqladmin status         | uptime, threads, queries        |
| mysql_secure_installation | harden a fresh install          |
| FLUSH PRIVILEGES          | reload grant tables             |
